Z||000008||RFMike||10-18-1999||09:44 PM||||As a show car, an 8 or 9.  As a car to drive on a daily basis, I have mixed reactions.  This would be a head turner, but let's see: lowered roof by 3", added a 3" wing below the roof line= 6" less rear vision.  Throw in the  4" of rear deck induction cowl, and now you've got 10" less rear vision than a stock Fiero GT, which sucks to start with.  If you drive every day in heavy traffic, you have to see in every direction.<BR>That said, I have to give the guy his due.  This had to be a huge undertaking.  Gotta respect that, even if I prefer stock.||||reg||1
Z||000009||SCCA FIERO||10-18-1999||11:01 PM||||That thing is AWESOME!!! It has the lines of a Vector and a Fiero...BITCHIN!! I give it a big fat <B>10</B><P>I think this is the only 87 GT I have ever seen converted to a notchback.||||reg||14
Z||000010||chris6878||10-19-1999||12:00 AM||||Mustang Killer???  That monster could be a Viper Killer...    Wow...   Look Out...<P>Gets a "10" in my book..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||10-19-1999||02:02 AM||||$550?? ouch.<P>anyone who has done any fiberglass work, like fabricating parts, I have a couple questions... <P>Assuming a person had the stock panels to work from, how hard would it be to make new panels (or modify stock ones) that looked like the IRM ones?<P>The only methods I know about are using a mold, which would require the original IRM panels, or the foam sandwich method.. which would probably be too thick. or what about making a mockup out of foam, and then taking a mold off that?<p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 10-19-1999).]||||reg||1
